# minimum required Qore version
%requires qore >= 1.8

# requires the SqlUtil module
%requires SqlUtil >= 1.7.5

# compare_version()
%requires Util >= 1.0

# don't use "$" signs for variables and class members, assume local variable scope
%modern
# a table holds its column, index, constraint and trigger descriptions with "@=" so that
# dereferencing a table does not scan them; see AbstractTable's members for the invariant
%allow-opaque-references

# require type definitions everywhere

# enable all warnings

module Sqlite3SqlUtil {
    version = "1.2";
    desc = "user module for working with Sqlite3 SQL data";
    author = "David Nichols <david@qore.org>";
    url = "http://qore.org";
    license = "MIT";
}

/** @mainpage Sqlite3SqlUtil Module

    @tableofcontents

    @section sqlite3sqlutilintro Sqlite3SqlUtil Module Introduction

    The %Sqlite3SqlUtil module provides a high level API for working with Sqlite3 database objects.

    Usually this module is loaded on demand from the SqlUtil module, however to use this module
    directly, use \c "%requires Sqlite3SqlUtil" in your code.

    All the public symbols in the module are defined in the Sqlite3SqlUtil namespace

    @note
    - This module requires the <a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/module-sqlite3">sqlite3</a> binary module
      for communication with Sqlite3 databases
    - This module does not yet support schema management for sqlite3 DBs

    @section sqlite_schema_management Schema Management on Sqlite3

    @subsection sqlite3_type_mapping Type Mapping

    Column types are mapped from %Qore types as follows:

    <b>Sqlite3 Column Type Mappings</b>
    |!Generic Type Name|!Sqlite3 Type Used
    |\c int|\c integer
    |\c float|\c real
    |\c number|\c real
    |\c string|\c text
    |\c date|\c text (iso-8601 format with millisecond precision)
    |\c binary|\c blob
    |\c bool|\c integer
    |@ref SqlUtil::BLOB|\c blob
    |@ref SqlUtil::CHAR|\c text
    |@ref SqlUtil::CLOB|\c text
    |@ref SqlUtil::NUMERIC|\c real
    |@ref SqlUtil::VARCHAR|\c text

    SQLite does not limit the length of text, so the size of a string column (\c string, @ref SqlUtil::CHAR,
    @ref SqlUtil::VARCHAR, or the native \c text and \c varchar types), as in
    <tt>{"qore_type": "string", "size": 96}</tt>, is accepted and is not part of the column: the column is created as
    \c text (or \c varchar), and is described from the database without a size, so a description with a size is
    aligned with no change.

    @subsection sqlite3_column_changes Changing Columns

    SQLite can change only one thing about a column of an existing table: its \c NOT \c NULL constraint, and only from
    SQLite 3.53.0 (@ref Sqlite3SqlUtil::SQLITE_ALTER_COLUMN_NOT_NULL_VERSION), with
    <tt>ALTER TABLE ... ALTER COLUMN ... SET NOT NULL</tt> and <tt>... DROP NOT NULL</tt>.

    So when a column of an existing table is modified, as by
    @ref SqlUtil::AbstractTable::getModifyColumnSql() "AbstractTable::getModifyColumnSql()" or by aligning a table
    to a template (@ref SqlUtil::AbstractTable::getAlignSql() "AbstractTable::getAlignSql()"):
    - a change of the \c NOT \c NULL constraint only is made with \c ALTER \c COLUMN when the SQLite library the
      sqlite3 module uses is 3.53.0 or later; making a column \c NOT \c NULL fails if a row holds \c NULL in it
    - with an older SQLite library, the same change raises \c UNSUPPORTED; an alignment raises it only if it makes
      no change that SQLite cannot make with any version (see @ref sqlite3_alignment_refusals)
    - any other change, of the column's type, its default value, or whether it is an auto-increment or primary key
      column, raises \c UNSUPPORTED with every SQLite version

    The version that counts is the SQLite library's, as the datasource reports it
    (@ref Qore::SQL::AbstractDatasource::getServerVersion() "AbstractDatasource::getServerVersion()"), not the
    version of the \c sqlite3 module.  A change that raises \c UNSUPPORTED can only be made by creating the table
    anew and copying its rows, which this module does not do.

    @subsection sqlite3_alignment_refusals Changes That No SQLite Version Can Make

    When an existing table is aligned to a template
    (@ref SqlUtil::AbstractTable::getAlignSql() "AbstractTable::getAlignSql()"), these changes raise \c UNSUPPORTED
    with every SQLite version:
    - adding a primary key to the table, or dropping its primary key (see @ref sqlite3_primary_keys)
    - adding a foreign constraint to the table, or dropping or changing one (see @ref sqlite3_foreign_constraints)
    - dropping or changing a unique constraint that the table was created with, or replacing it with a unique index
      (see @ref sqlite3_unique_constraints)
    - changing a column's type, its default value, or whether it is an auto-increment or primary key column (see
      @ref sqlite3_column_changes)

    The alignment raises them before it changes a column, so a change of a column's \c NOT \c NULL constraint, which
    an SQLite library older than 3.53.0 cannot make either, raises \c UNSUPPORTED only if the alignment makes none of
    them, and an alignment reports the same change with every SQLite version.  For example, a template that adds a
    primary key to a table and declares the key's column \c NOT \c NULL, where the table's column is nullable, raises
    \c UNSUPPORTED for the primary key with every SQLite version, not for the column's \c NOT \c NULL constraint.

    @subsection sqlite3_primary_keys Primary Keys

    SQLite creates a primary key only with its table: a table's primary key is written in its \c CREATE \c TABLE
    statement as a \c PRIMARY \c KEY table constraint, with the key's columns in the key's order, so
    @ref SqlUtil::AbstractTable::create() "AbstractTable::create()" and table alignment create a table with its
    primary key.  A primary key of one column of type \c integer is the table's rowid, so a row inserted without a
    value for it is given one.

    SQLite has no name for a primary key, so the name given in a table description is not kept, and renaming a
    primary key needs no SQL.  SQLite cannot add a primary key to an existing table or drop the primary key of one,
    so aligning an existing table to a template with another primary key raises \c UNSUPPORTED with a description,
    with every SQLite version, also when the template changes a column too (see @ref sqlite3_alignment_refusals);
    such a table can only be created anew with the new key and its rows copied, which this module does not do.  A
    table created by Sqlite3SqlUtil 1.1 or earlier was created without its primary key, so aligning it to its
    template raises \c UNSUPPORTED in the same way.

    @subsection sqlite3_foreign_constraints Foreign Constraints

    SQLite also creates a foreign constraint only with its table: each foreign constraint of a table is written in
    its \c CREATE \c TABLE statement as a table constraint, <tt>constraint</tt> <em>name</em>
    <tt>foreign key (</tt>...<tt>) references</tt> <em>table</em> <tt>(</tt>...<tt>)</tt>, with the source and
    target columns in the constraint's order.  The referenced table does not have to exist when the table is
    created, so the tables of a schema can be created in any order.

    SQLite does not report the name of a foreign constraint: the name given in a table description is written in the
    \c CREATE \c TABLE statement, but a foreign constraint that is read from the database (as by
    @ref SqlUtil::AbstractTable::getForeignConstraints() "AbstractTable::getForeignConstraints()" or
    @ref SqlUtil::AbstractTable::getDescriptionHash() "AbstractTable::getDescriptionHash()") is named
    <tt>sqlite_fk_</tt><em>n</em>, where \em n is SQLite's number for the constraint in its table.  So a table is
    aligned to a template by the definitions of its foreign constraints, their source columns, referenced table and
    referenced columns, not by their names: a foreign constraint with the same definition under another name is
    unchanged and needs no SQL.  Do not name a foreign constraint <tt>sqlite_fk_</tt><em>n</em> yourself.

    SQLite cannot add a foreign constraint to an existing table or drop one, so aligning an existing table to a
    template with other foreign constraints raises \c UNSUPPORTED with a description, with every SQLite version (see
    @ref sqlite3_alignment_refusals); a changed foreign constraint is one dropped and another added.  Such a table can
    only be created anew and its rows copied, which this module does not do.

    A foreign constraint description in SqlUtil has no referential actions (\c ON \c DELETE, \c ON \c UPDATE) on
    any database, so a foreign constraint is created without them and compared without them, and a foreign
    constraint on SQLite cannot be deferrable through SqlUtil.

    @note Foreign constraints are declared but not enforced on SQLite through the \c sqlite3 driver: a row that
    references no row of the referenced table can be inserted.  SQLite enforces foreign constraints only on a
    connection on which <tt>PRAGMA foreign_keys</tt> is on; it is off by default, and SQLite ignores the pragma inside
    a transaction, while the \c sqlite3 driver runs every statement in a transaction, so the pragma cannot be turned
    on through the driver.  Only an SQLite library built with \c SQLITE_DEFAULT_FOREIGN_KEYS=1 enforces them.  This
    module does not change the setting.

    @subsection sqlite3_unique_constraints Unique Constraints

    SQLite creates a unique constraint with its table: each unique constraint of a table is written in its
    \c CREATE \c TABLE statement as a table constraint,
    <tt>constraint</tt> <em>name</em> <tt>unique (</tt>...<tt>)</tt>, with its columns in the constraint's order, and
    SQLite enforces it with an automatic unique index, which cannot be dropped.  SQLite does not report the name of
    such a constraint: a unique constraint that is read from the database is named as SQLite names its automatic
    index, <tt>sqlite_autoindex_</tt><em>table</em><tt>_</tt><em>n</em>, and the automatic index is part of the
    constraint, not an index of the table, as the automatic index of a primary key is.

    A unique index on columns enforces the same constraint: as SQLite treats them alike, a unique index of a table is
    also described as a unique constraint of the table, with the index's name, and a unique index in a table
    description is also a unique constraint of the table.  A partial unique index, or one on an expression, is
    described as an index only.

    SQLite cannot add a unique table constraint to an existing table, so aligning an existing table to a template with
    a new unique constraint creates a unique index with the constraint's name
    (<tt>create unique index</tt> <em>name</em> <tt>on</tt> <em>table</em> <tt>(</tt>...<tt>)</tt>), which the next
    alignment reads back as the same constraint and leaves as it is.  A unique constraint that is a unique index is
    dropped by dropping its index, and changed by dropping its index and creating another.  A table is aligned to a
    template by the columns of its unique constraints, not by their names: a unique constraint with the same columns
    under another name is unchanged and needs no SQL.

    A unique constraint that a table was created with cannot be dropped or changed, nor be replaced with a unique
    index on its columns, so aligning an existing table to a template without it raises \c UNSUPPORTED with a
    description, with every SQLite version (see @ref sqlite3_alignment_refusals); such a table can only be created
    anew and its rows copied, which this module does not do.

    @section sqlite3_relnotes Release Notes

    @subsection sqlite3sqlutilv12 Sqlite3SqlUtil_1_2 Sqlite3SqlUtil Module v1.2
    - renaming a trigger raised \c UNSUPPORTED: SQLite cannot rename a trigger, so it is dropped and created under
      the new name
    - a string column with a size, as in a portable description (<tt>{"qore_type": "string", "size": 96}</tt>), is
      accepted: SQLite does not limit the length of text, so the size is not part of the column; it raised
      \c COLUMN-ERROR, so that such a description, such as the quarantine table of a DataSchema \c reconcile_table
      migration, could not be used on SQLite (see @ref sqlite3_type_mapping)
    - a column's \c NOT \c NULL constraint can be set or dropped on SQLite 3.53.0 and later, as by
      @ref SqlUtil::AbstractTable::getModifyColumnSql() "AbstractTable::getModifyColumnSql()" or table alignment;
      every column change raised \c UNSUPPORTED before.  A change that SQLite cannot make, or a \c NOT \c NULL
      change on an older SQLite, still raises \c UNSUPPORTED, now with a description of what cannot be changed;
      see @ref sqlite3_column_changes
    - a select with the \c forupdate @ref select_option_hash "select option" now raises
      \c FORUPDATE-NOT-SUPPORTED, since SQLite cannot lock rows; it failed with an SQL syntax error before
    - fixed tables reporting no primary key, so \c upsert() always failed with \c UPSERT-ERROR; the primary key is
      now read from the table's description
    - fixed tables being created without their primary key, which was dropped from the \c CREATE \c TABLE
      statement, so that a primary key was not enforced, \c upsert() failed, and aligning the table to its own
      template raised a bare \c UNSUPPORTED, as every schema upgrade did for a table with a primary key; the
      primary key is now created with its table, and adding a primary key to an existing table or dropping it raises
      \c UNSUPPORTED with a description of what SQLite cannot change; see @ref sqlite3_primary_keys
    - fixed creating a table with foreign constraints, which failed with an SQL syntax error, since SQLite cannot add
      a foreign constraint to an existing table; foreign constraints are now created with their table, described
      from SQLite's foreign key pragma, and aligned by their definitions, as SQLite does not report their names;
      adding a foreign constraint to an existing table or dropping one raises \c UNSUPPORTED with a description;
      foreign constraints are declared but not enforced through the \c sqlite3 driver; see
      @ref sqlite3_foreign_constraints
    - fixed renaming an index, as table alignment does for an index with the definition of the template's index
      under another name: the index was dropped and not created again, as the SQL that drops it and creates it under
      the new name was one string, of which the \c sqlite3 driver runs only the first statement; SQLite cannot rename
      an index, so such an index is now taken for the template's index and kept under its name in the database,
      without rebuilding it, and is dropped by that name (see
      @ref SqlUtil::AbstractIndex::supportsRename() "AbstractIndex::supportsRename()")
    - fixed the SQL that creates an index with a column collation, which had an empty \c COLLATE clause
    - fixed table descriptions with a unique constraint or a unique index, which raised a bare \c UNSUPPORTED when
      they were set up, so that such a table could be neither created nor aligned; a unique constraint is now created
      with its table, described from SQLite's index pragmas under the name of its automatic index, which is no longer
      described as an index of the table, added to an existing table as a unique index, and aligned by its columns;
      a unique index is also a unique constraint of its table, as in SQLite; dropping or changing a unique constraint
      that a table was created with raises \c UNSUPPORTED with a description; see @ref sqlite3_unique_constraints
    - fixed table alignment reporting a different refusal on an SQLite library older than 3.53.0 than on a newer
      one: a template that made a change that no SQLite version can make, such as adding a primary key, and changed
      a column's \c NOT \c NULL constraint, such as by declaring the key's column \c NOT \c NULL, raised
      \c UNSUPPORTED for the \c NOT \c NULL constraint on the older library; the changes that no SQLite version can
      make are now refused before a column is changed, so an alignment reports the same change with every SQLite
      version; see @ref sqlite3_alignment_refusals
    - fixed describing indexes: a table with an automatic index (a composite primary key or a unique
      constraint) failed, a unique index created in lower case was reported as not unique, and quoted column
      names kept their quotes; indexes are now described from SQLite's index pragmas instead of their SQL
    - fixed duplicate-row detection, which matched another database's error message and so never recognized a
      duplicate key; a primary key or unique constraint violation is now recognized from the result code the
      sqlite3 driver reports, or from SQLite's own message with an older driver
    - added temporary-table support (\c "create temp table"); see
      @ref SqlUtil::AbstractDatabase::createTempTable() "AbstractDatabase::createTempTable()"
    - made SQL \c like, \c contains, \c startsWith, and \c endsWith expression behavior honor the
      DataProvider case-sensitivity contract instead of inheriting SQLite's case-insensitive ASCII \c LIKE
      behavior; SQL LIKE wildcard translation now also preserves literal SQLite GLOB metacharacters

    @subsection sqlite3sqlutilv11 Sqlite3SqlUtil_1_1 Sqlite3SqlUtil Module v1.1
    - implemented support for generating queries based on generic expressions using the DataProvider module's generic
      expression support
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/4538">issue 4538</a>)

    @subsection sqlite3sqlutilv10 Sqlite3SqlUtil_1_0 Sqlite3SqlUtil Module v1.0
    - initial release
*/

#! the Sqlite3SqlUtil namespace contains all the objects in the Sqlite3SqlUtil module
public namespace Sqlite3SqlUtil {
    #! SQLite's extended result code for a primary key constraint violation
    public const SQLITE_CONSTRAINT_PRIMARYKEY = 1555;
    #! SQLite's extended result code for a unique constraint violation
    public const SQLITE_CONSTRAINT_UNIQUE = 2067;
    #! The first SQLite version that can set or drop the \c NOT \c NULL constraint of a column of an existing table
    /** @see @ref sqlite3_column_changes

        @since Sqlite3SqlUtil 1.2
    */
    public const SQLITE_ALTER_COLUMN_NOT_NULL_VERSION = "3.53.0";

    #! returns a Sqlite3Table object corresponding to the arguments
    public Sqlite3Table sub get_table(AbstractDatasource nds, string nname, *hash<auto> opts) {
        return new Sqlite3Table(nds, nname, opts);
    }

    #! returns a Sqlite3Database object corresponding to the arguments
    public Sqlite3Database sub get_database(AbstractDatasource nds, *hash<auto> opts) {
        return new Sqlite3Database(nds, opts);
    }

    #! Sqlite3 savepoint helper
    public class Sqlite3SavepointHelper inherits AbstractSavepointHelper {
        #! Creates the object
        constructor(AbstractDatasource ds, *string savepoint) : AbstractSavepointHelper(ds, savepoint) {
        }

        #! Creates the savepoint
        private createSavepointImpl() {
            ds.exec("savepoint " + savepoint);
        }

        #! Rolls back to the savepoint
        private rollbackImpl() {
            ds.exec("rollback to " + savepoint);
        }

        #! Deletes the savepoint
        private deleteSavepointImpl() {
            ds.exec("release " + savepoint);
        }
    }

    #! represents a Sqlite3-specific index
    public class Sqlite3Index inherits SqlUtil::AbstractIndex {
        public {
            hash<string, string> collations;
        }

        #! creates the object from the arguments
        constructor(string name, bool unique, hash<auto> cols, *hash<string, string> collations)
                : AbstractIndex(name, unique, cols) {
            if (collations) {
                self.collations = collations;
            }
        }

        #! returns a string that can be used to create the index in the database
        string getCreateSql(string table_name, *hash<auto> opt) {
            return sprintf("create %sindex %s on %s (%s)", unique ? "unique " : "", name, table_name,
                (foldl $1 + ", " + $2, getColumnCollationList()));
        }

        #! Returns a list of column name with COLLATE argument per column, if appropriate
        /** @return the SQL name of each column of the index, in the index's order, followed by
            <tt>COLLATE</tt> <em>collation</em> for a column with a collation
        */
        list<string> getColumnCollationList() {
            list<string> rv = ();
            foreach hash<auto> i in (columns.pairIterator()) {
                string sql_name = i.value.ddl_name ?? i.key;
                *string collation = collations{i.key};
                rv += collation ? sprintf("%s COLLATE %s", sql_name, collation) : sql_name;
            }
            return rv;
        }

        #! returns @ref True "True" if the argument is equal to the current index, @ref False "False" if not
        private bool equalImpl(AbstractIndex ix) {
            return True;
        }

        #! returns no SQL: SQLite cannot rename an index
        /** An index is never dropped and created again under another name; see @ref supportsRename()
        */
        softlist<string> getRenameSql(string table_name, string new_name) {
            return ();
        }

        #! returns @ref False "False": SQLite cannot rename an index
        /** Table alignment takes an index with the definition of a template index under another name for the
            template's index, and keeps it under its name in the database, which the SQL that drops it uses (see
            @ref SqlUtil::AbstractIndex::supportsRename() "AbstractIndex::supportsRename()" and
            @ref SqlUtil::AbstractIndex::getDbName() "AbstractIndex::getDbName()")

            @since Sqlite3SqlUtil 1.2
        */
        bool supportsRename() {
            return False;
        }

        #! returns a string that can be used to drop the index from the table, by its name in the database
        string getDropSql(string table_name) {
            return sprintf("drop index %s", getDbName());
        }
    }

    #! represents a Sqlite3-specific foreign constraint
    /** SQLite creates a foreign constraint only with its table: @ref Sqlite3Table writes it in the table's
        \c CREATE \c TABLE statement (@ref getTableConstraintSql()), and a foreign constraint cannot be added to or
        dropped from an existing table; SQLite does not report the name of a foreign constraint, so a described one is
        named by @ref Sqlite3Table; see @ref sqlite3_foreign_constraints
    */
    public class Sqlite3ForeignConstraint inherits SqlUtil::AbstractForeignConstraint {
        constructor(string n, Columns c, ForeignConstraintTarget t) : AbstractForeignConstraint(n, c, t) {
        }

        #! returns the \c FOREIGN \c KEY table constraint that creates the foreign constraint with its table
        /** @par Example:
            @code{.py}
# "constraint fk_order_item_order foreign key (order_id) references orders (id)"
string sql = fk.getTableConstraintSql();
            @endcode

            @return the \c FOREIGN \c KEY table constraint, named with the constraint's name, with the source and
            target columns in the constraint's order

            @since Sqlite3SqlUtil 1.2
        */
        string getTableConstraintSql() {
            return sprintf("constraint %s foreign key (%s) references %s (%s)", name,
                (foldl $1 + ", " + $2, getSqlColumnNameIterator()), target.table,
                (foldl $1 + ", " + $2, target.columns.getSqlColumnNameIterator()));
        }

        #! SQLite cannot add a foreign constraint to an existing table
        /** The foreign constraints of a table are created with the table (@ref getTableConstraintSql())

            @throw UNSUPPORTED SQLite cannot add a foreign constraint to an existing table
        */
        string getCreateSql(string table_name, *hash<auto> opt) {
            return getCreateSql(name, table_name, opt);
        }

        #! returns an empty list: SQLite does not keep the name of a foreign constraint, so there is nothing to rename
        /** @return an empty list
        */
        softlist getRenameSql(string table_name, string new_name) {
            return ();
        }

        #! returns @ref False "False": SQLite does not keep the name of a foreign constraint
        /** A foreign constraint is aligned by its definition (see @ref sqlite3_foreign_constraints and
            @ref SqlUtil::AbstractConstraint::supportsRename() "AbstractConstraint::supportsRename()")

            @since Sqlite3SqlUtil 1.2
        */
        bool supportsRename() {
            return False;
        }

        #! SQLite cannot add a foreign constraint to an existing table
        /** The foreign constraints of a table are created with the table (@ref getTableConstraintSql())

            @throw UNSUPPORTED SQLite cannot add a foreign constraint to an existing table
        */
        string getCreateSql(string name, string table_name, *hash<auto> opt) {
            throw "UNSUPPORTED", sprintf("cannot add the foreign constraint %y (%s) referencing %s (%s) to table %y: "
                "SQLite cannot add a foreign constraint to an existing table; a table has the foreign constraints "
                "that it was created with", name, getColumnListDesc(), target.table, getTargetColumnListDesc(),
                table_name);
        }

        string getAddSql(string name, string table_name, *hash<auto> opt) {
            return sprintf("add constraint %s foreign key (%s) references %s (%s)", name,
                (foldl $1 + ", " + $2, getSqlColumnNameIterator()), target.table,
                (foldl $1 + ", " + $2, target.columns.getSqlColumnNameIterator()));
        }

        #! SQLite cannot drop a foreign constraint of an existing table
        /** @throw UNSUPPORTED SQLite cannot drop a foreign constraint of an existing table
        */
        string getDropSql(string table_name) {
            throw "UNSUPPORTED", sprintf("cannot drop the foreign constraint (%s) referencing %s (%s) of table %y: "
                "SQLite cannot drop a foreign constraint of an existing table; a table has the foreign constraints "
                "that it was created with", getColumnListDesc(), target.table, getTargetColumnListDesc(), table_name);
        }

        #! returns the source columns, separated by commas, for a description
        private string getColumnListDesc() {
            return join(", ", self.keys());
        }

        #! returns the target columns, separated by commas, for a description
        private string getTargetColumnListDesc() {
            return join(", ", target.columns.keys());
        }
    }

    #! represents a Sqlite3-specific column
    public class Sqlite3Column inherits SqlUtil::AbstractColumn {
        public {
            bool auto_increment;
            bool primary_key;
        }

        #! empty constructor for subclasses
        private constructor() {
        }

        #! Creates the column
        constructor(string name, string native_type, *string qore_type, bool nullable, *string default_value,
                bool auto_increment = False, bool primary_key = False)
                : AbstractColumn(name, native_type, qore_type, 0, nullable, default_value) {
            self.auto_increment = auto_increment;
            self.primary_key = primary_key;
        }

        #! returns a list of sql strings that can be used to add the column to an existing table
        /** @par Example:
            @code{.py}
list<string> l = col.getAddColumnSql(t);
            @endcode

            @param t the AbstractTable object to modify
         */
        softlist<string> getAddColumnSql(AbstractTable t) {
            return sprintf("alter table %s add column %s", t.getSqlName(), AbstractColumn::getCreateSql(t));
        }

        #! returns a list of sql strings that can be used to modify the column to the new definition
        /** if the column definitions are identical then an empty list is returned

            SQLite can change only the \c NOT \c NULL constraint of an existing column, and only from SQLite
            3.53.0 (@ref SQLITE_ALTER_COLUMN_NOT_NULL_VERSION); see @ref sqlite3_column_changes

            @par Example:
            @code{.py}
list l = col.getModifySql(t, newcol);
            @endcode

            The column names are assumed to be equal.

            @param t the AbstractTable object to modify
            @param col the new column definition
            @param opt column modification options (none are supported in this class)

            @return a list of sql strings that can be used to modify the column to the new definition; if the column
            definitions are identical then an empty list is returned

            @throw UNSUPPORTED the new definition changes the column's type, default value, comment, or whether it
            is an auto-increment or primary key column, which SQLite cannot change; or it changes only the column's
            \c NOT \c NULL constraint, and the SQLite library is older than 3.53.0
         */
        softlist<string> getModifySqlImpl(AbstractTable t, AbstractColumn col, *hash<auto> opt) {
            checkModify(t, col);
            Sqlite3Column c = cast<Sqlite3Column>(col);
            if (nullable == c.nullable) {
                return ();
            }
            string action = c.nullable ? "drop" : "set";
            string version = t.getDatasource().getServerVersion();
            if (compare_version(version, SQLITE_ALTER_COLUMN_NOT_NULL_VERSION) < 0) {
                throw "UNSUPPORTED", sprintf("cannot %s the NOT NULL constraint of column %y of table %y: SQLite %s "
                    "cannot change a column of an existing table; SQLite %s or later is required", action, name,
                    t.getName(), version, SQLITE_ALTER_COLUMN_NOT_NULL_VERSION);
            }
            return sprintf("alter table %s alter column %s %s not null", t.getSqlName(), ddl_name, action);
        }

        #! raises an exception if the new definition changes what no SQLite version can change in a column
        /** SQLite can change only the \c NOT \c NULL constraint of a column of an existing table, and only from
            SQLite 3.53.0 (@ref SQLITE_ALTER_COLUMN_NOT_NULL_VERSION); this method checks the changes that SQLite
            cannot make with any version, and not the \c NOT \c NULL constraint; see @ref sqlite3_column_changes

            @par Example:
            @code{.py}
col.checkModify(t, newcol);
            @endcode

            The column names are assumed to be equal.

            @param t the AbstractTable object to modify
            @param col the new column definition

            @throw UNSUPPORTED the new definition changes the column's type, default value, comment, or whether it
            is an auto-increment or primary key column, which SQLite cannot change

            @since Sqlite3SqlUtil 1.2
         */
        checkModify(AbstractTable t, AbstractColumn col) {
            Sqlite3Column c = cast<Sqlite3Column>(col);
            list<string> changes;
            if (native_type != c.native_type || size != c.size || (scale ?? 0) != (c.scale ?? 0)) {
                changes += "type";
            }
            if (def_val != c.def_val) {
                changes += "default value";
            }
            if (comment != c.comment) {
                changes += "comment";
            }
            if (auto_increment != c.auto_increment) {
                changes += "auto increment";
            }
            if (primary_key != c.primary_key) {
                changes += "primary key";
            }
            if (changes) {
                string what = changes.size() == 1
                    ? changes[0]
                    : join(", ", changes[0..changes.size() - 2]) + " or " + changes.last();
                throw "UNSUPPORTED", sprintf("cannot modify column %y of table %y: SQLite cannot change the %s of a "
                    "column of an existing table; it can change only its NOT NULL constraint", name, t.getName(),
                    what);
            }
        }

        #! returns a string that can be used to rename the column
        /** @par Example:
            @code{.py}
string str = col.getRenameSql(t, "new_name");
            @endcode

            @param t the AbstractTable object to modify
            @param new_name the new name for the column

            @return a string that can be used to rename the column
        */
        string getRenameSql(AbstractTable t, string new_name) {
            return sprintf("alter table %s rename column %s to %s", t.getSqlName(), ddl_name, getDdlName(new_name));
        }

        #! returns the column name with quoting in case the column name is a reserved word
        /** the name returned here will be used when executing DDL
        */
        string getDdlName(string name) {
            return Sqlite3Database::Sqlite3ReservedWords{name.lwr()} ? ("'" + name + "'") : name;
        }

        #! returns @ref True "True" if the argument is equal to the current object, @ref False "False" if not
        private bool equalImpl(AbstractColumn c) {
            Sqlite3Column sc = c;
            return auto_increment == sc.auto_increment && primary_key == sc.primary_key;
        }
    }

    #! represents a Sqlite3-specific unique constraint
    /** SQLite creates a unique constraint with its table: @ref Sqlite3Table writes it in the table's
        \c CREATE \c TABLE statement (@ref getTableConstraintSql()), and SQLite enforces it with an automatic unique
        index that cannot be dropped.  A unique constraint is added to an existing table as a unique index
        (@ref getCreateSql()), which SQLite enforces in the same way and which can be dropped.  SQLite does not report
        the name of a unique constraint created with its table, so a described one is named by @ref Sqlite3Table; see
        @ref sqlite3_unique_constraints
    */
    public class Sqlite3UniqueConstraint inherits SqlUtil::AbstractUniqueConstraint {
        private {
            #! @ref True "True" if the constraint was created with its table, which SQLite cannot drop it from
            bool table_constraint;
        }

        #! creates the object from the arguments
        /** @param name the name of the constraint
            @param cols the columns of the constraint, in the constraint's order
            @param table_constraint @ref True "True" if the constraint is described from a table that was created with
            it, as a \c UNIQUE table constraint, which SQLite cannot drop; @ref False "False" for a constraint of a
            table description, or one described from a unique index
            @param index the name of the unique index of the constraint, if it is described from one

            @since Sqlite3SqlUtil 1.2 the \a table_constraint and \a index arguments
        */
        constructor(string name, hash<auto> cols, bool table_constraint = False, *string index)
                : AbstractUniqueConstraint(name, cols, index) {
            self.table_constraint = table_constraint;
        }

        #! returns the Sqlite3Column value of the given key if it exists, otherwise throws a \c KEY-ERROR exception
        /** @par Example:
            @code{.py}
auto v = c.name;
            @endcode

            @param k the name of the key to access

            @return the value of the given key in the contained hash if it exists

            @throw KEY-ERROR the given key does not exist in the contained hash

            @note this method is called automatically when an unknown or inaccessible member name is accessed from
            outside the class

            @see @ref memberGate_methods
        */
        Sqlite3Column memberGate(string k) {
            if (!h.hasKey(k)) {
                throw "KEY-ERROR", sprintf("cannot access unknown %s %y; expecting one of: %y", getElementName(), k,
                    h.keys());
            }
            return h{k};
        }

        #! returns @ref True "True" if the constraint was created with its table, which SQLite cannot drop it from
        /** @return @ref True "True" if the constraint is described from a table that was created with it, as a
            \c UNIQUE table constraint; @ref False "False" if it is described from a unique index or is a constraint of
            a table description

            @since Sqlite3SqlUtil 1.2
        */
        bool isTableConstraint() {
            return table_constraint;
        }

        #! returns the \c UNIQUE table constraint that creates the constraint with its table
        /** @par Example:
            @code{.py}
# "constraint uk_order_number unique (customer_id, 'order')"
string sql = uk.getTableConstraintSql();
            @endcode

            @return the \c UNIQUE table constraint, named with the constraint's name, with its columns in the
            constraint's order

            @since Sqlite3SqlUtil 1.2
        */
        string getTableConstraintSql() {
            return sprintf("constraint %s unique (%s)", name, (foldl $1 + ", " + $2, getSqlColumnNameIterator()));
        }

        #! returns the SQL that adds the constraint to an existing table: a unique index with the constraint's name
        /** SQLite cannot add a \c UNIQUE table constraint to an existing table; a unique index enforces the same
            constraint, and is described as the same constraint (see @ref sqlite3_unique_constraints)

            @par Example:
            @code{.py}
# "create unique index uk_order_number on orders (customer_id, 'order')"
string sql = uk.getCreateSql("orders");
            @endcode

            @param table_name the name of the table
            @param opts this argument is ignored

            @return the \c CREATE \c UNIQUE \c INDEX statement that adds the constraint to an existing table
        */
        string getCreateSql(string table_name, *hash<auto> opts) {
            return getCreateSql(name, table_name, opts);
        }

        #! returns the SQL that adds the constraint to an existing table under the given name: a unique index
        /** @param name the name of the constraint and its index
            @param table_name the name of the table
            @param opts this argument is ignored

            @return the \c CREATE \c UNIQUE \c INDEX statement that adds the constraint to an existing table
        */
        string getCreateSql(string name, string table_name, *hash<auto> opts) {
            return sprintf("create unique index %s on %s (%s)", name, table_name,
                (foldl $1 + ", " + $2, getSqlColumnNameIterator()));
        }

        #! returns an empty list: a unique constraint in SQLite is matched by its columns, not by its name
        /** SQLite does not keep the name of a unique constraint created with its table, and a table is aligned to a
            template by the columns of its unique constraints (see @ref sqlite3_unique_constraints)

            @return an empty list; there is nothing to rename
        */
        list<string> getRenameSql(string table_name, string new_name) {
            return ();
        }

        #! returns @ref False "False": a unique constraint in SQLite cannot be renamed
        /** A unique constraint created with its table has no name in SQLite, and one that is a unique index cannot be
            renamed either; a unique constraint is aligned by its columns (see @ref sqlite3_unique_constraints and
            @ref SqlUtil::AbstractConstraint::supportsRename() "AbstractConstraint::supportsRename()")

            @since Sqlite3SqlUtil 1.2
        */
        bool supportsRename() {
            return False;
        }

        #! returns the SQL that drops the constraint's unique index from the table
        /** @param table_name the name of the table

            @return the \c DROP \c INDEX statement that drops the constraint's unique index

            @throw UNSUPPORTED the constraint was created with its table, which SQLite cannot drop it from
        */
        string getDropSql(string table_name) {
            if (table_constraint) {
                throw "UNSUPPORTED", sprintf("cannot drop the unique constraint (%s) of table %y: SQLite cannot drop a "
                    "unique constraint that a table was created with", join(", ", self.keys()), table_name);
            }
            return sprintf("drop index %s", index ?? name);
        }

        #! returns no SQL: the constraint is its unique index, so recreating the index recreates the constraint
        /** @param ds this argument is ignored
            @param table_name this argument is ignored
            @param opts this argument is ignored

            @return a hash with empty \c disable and \c enable lists
        */
        hash<auto> getDisableReenableSql(AbstractDatasource ds, string table_name, *hash<auto> opts) {
            return {"disable": (), "enable": ()};
        }

        #! finds the unique index of the constraint, unless the constraint was created with its table
        /** The automatic index of a constraint that was created with its table is part of the constraint, and is not
            described as an index, so it is never another unique index of the table with the same columns

            @param indexes the indexes of the table
        */
        findMatchingIndex(*Indexes indexes) {
            if (!table_constraint) {
                AbstractColumnSupportingConstraint::findMatchingIndex(indexes);
            }
        }
    }

    #! represents a Sqlite3-specific primary key constraint
    /** SQLite creates a primary key only with its table: @ref Sqlite3Table writes it in the table's
        \c CREATE \c TABLE statement (@ref getTableConstraintSql()), and a primary key cannot be added to or dropped
        from an existing table; see @ref sqlite3_primary_keys
    */
    public class Sqlite3PrimaryKey inherits SqlUtil::AbstractPrimaryKey {
        constructor() {
        }

        constructor(hash<auto> c) : AbstractPrimaryKey("PRIMARY", c) {
        }

        #! returns the \c PRIMARY \c KEY table constraint that creates the primary key with its table
        /** @par Example:
            @code{.py}
# "primary key ('group', id)"
string sql = pk.getTableConstraintSql();
            @endcode

            @return the \c PRIMARY \c KEY table constraint, with the key's columns in the key's order

            @since Sqlite3SqlUtil 1.2
        */
        string getTableConstraintSql() {
            return sprintf("primary key (%s)", (foldl $1 + ", " + $2, getSqlColumnNameIterator()));
        }

        #! SQLite cannot add a primary key to an existing table
        /** The primary key of a table is created with the table (@ref getTableConstraintSql())

            @throw UNSUPPORTED SQLite cannot add a primary key to an existing table
        */
        string getCreateSql(string table_name, *hash<auto> opts) {
            throw "UNSUPPORTED", sprintf("cannot add the primary key (%s) to table %y: SQLite cannot add a primary "
                "key to an existing table; a table has the primary key that it was created with",
                join(", ", self.keys()), table_name);
        }

        #! returns an empty list: a primary key in SQLite has no name (@ref supportsName())
        /** @return an empty list; there is nothing to rename
        */
        list<string> getRenameSql(string table_name, string new_name) {
            return ();
        }

        #! SQLite cannot drop the primary key of an existing table
        /** @throw UNSUPPORTED SQLite cannot drop the primary key of an existing table
        */
        string getDropSql(string table_name) {
            throw "UNSUPPORTED", sprintf("cannot drop the primary key (%s) of table %y: SQLite cannot drop the "
                "primary key of an existing table; a table has the primary key that it was created with",
                join(", ", self.keys()), table_name);
        }

        #! returns @ref False "False" since primary key constraints in Sqlite3 have no name
        bool supportsName() {
            return False;
        }
    }

    #! represents a Sqlite3-specific trigger
    public class Sqlite3Trigger inherits SqlUtil::AbstractTrigger {
        #! creates the object and sets its name and the trigger source
        constructor(string n, string n_src) : AbstractTrigger(n, n_src) {
        }

        #! returns a string that can be used to create the trigger in the database
        softlist getCreateSql(string table_name, *hash<auto> opt) {
            return sprintf("create trigger %s %s", name, src);
        }

        #! returns a string that can be used to drop the trigger from the database
        softlist getDropSql(string table_name) {
            return sprintf("drop trigger %s", name);
        }

        #! returns @ref True "True" if the argument is equal to the current object, @ref False "False" if not
        private bool equalImpl(AbstractFunctionBase t) {
            return True;
        }

        #! returns the SQL that renames the trigger: SQLite cannot rename a trigger, so it is dropped and created under the
        #! new name
        /** A trigger is only its definition, which is created again unchanged under the new name

            @param table_name the name of the table
            @param new_name the new name of the trigger

            @return the SQL statements that drop the trigger and create it under the new name
        */
        softlist getRenameSql(string table_name, string new_name) {
            return (getDropSql(table_name)[0], sprintf("create trigger %s %s", new_name, src));
        }
    }

    #! represents a Sqlite3 view
    public class Sqlite3View inherits SqlUtil::AbstractView {
        #! creates the object from the arguments
        constructor(string name, string src) : AbstractView(name, src) {
        }

        #! returns a string that can be used to create the view in the database
        string getCreateSql(*hash<auto> opt) {
            # TODO/FIXME: check options
            return sprintf("create view %s as %s", name, src);
        }

        #! returns a string that can be used to rename the view in the database
        /** @param new_name the new name of the object
            @param opt this argument is ignored
         */
        softlist getRenameSql(string new_name, *hash<auto> opt) {
            throw "UNSUPPORTED";
        }
    }

